  "statement": "For every supplied real rounding model with parameter u and every finite nonempty ordered full binary tree of real pairs, let d be the maximum root-to-leaf edge count. The construction rounds each leaf product once, then rounds the sum of the child results at every internal node according to the given tree. If (d+1)u<1, its output equals the exact sum of a real tree of the same ordered shape whose leaf at each original pair (a,b) differs from ab by at most \u03b3(d+1,u)|ab|, where \u03b3(k,u)=ku/(1-ku). The perturbations are of product values; the statement does not assert separately perturbed factors.",
  "targets": [
    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.roundedDotProduct_exists_perturbation",
      "statement": "For every model M\u2208Research.FloatingPoint.Model and every t\u2208Tree(\u211d\u00d7\u211d), put u=M.u, R=M.round, d=depth(t), and k=d+1\u2208\u2115. A model includes 0\u2264u and \u2200z\u2208\u211d, \u2203\u03b4\u2208\u211d such that |\u03b4|\u2264u and R(z)=z(1+\u03b4). Trees are finite ordered trees formed by a leaf carrying one value or a node with exactly two child trees. Depth is defined by depth(leaf(v))=0 and depth(node(l,r))=1+max(depth(l),depth(r)). If ku<1, with k coerced to a real in this inequality, then there exists s\u2208Tree(\u211d) such that IsPerturbation(\u03b3(k,u),exactProductTree(t),s) and exactSum(s)=roundedDotProduct(M,t). Here \u03b3(k,u)=ku/(1-ku); exactSum(leaf(x))=x and exactSum(node(l,r))=exactSum(l)+exactSum(r). The inductive relation IsPerturbation(B,leaf(p),leaf(q)) holds exactly when |q-p|\u2264B|p|; for nodes it holds exactly when the respective left and right children satisfy the same relation with the same bound B. No different shapes are related. Consequently s has the same ordered shape as t, with |q-ab|\u2264\u03b3(d+1,u)|ab| for each original leaf (a,b) and corresponding leaf q of s. The reference exactProductTree has leaf values ab, whereas roundedDotProduct is the actual scalar obtained by rounding leaf products and internal sums as described in the constructions. The strict hypothesis makes the denominator positive. The same maximum-depth bound applies to every leaf, including shallower ones. For a single leaf the depth is zero, the output is R(ab), and the bound is \u03b3(1,u). The conclusion is existential and concerns perturbed product values, with no assertion of separate perturbations of the factors."
    }
  ],
  "constructions": [
    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.exactProductTree",
      "description": "A function Tree(\u211d\u00d7\u211d)\u2192Tree(\u211d) mapping each leaf pair (a,b) to its exact real product ab, preserving every internal node and the order of its children. Recursively E(leaf((a,b)))=leaf(ab) and E(node(l,r))=node(E(l),E(r)). It performs no addition and returns a tree. This is the reference tree in the target theorem\u2019s perturbation relation."
    },
    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.roundedProductTree",
      "description": "For every model M and every t\u2208Tree(\u211d\u00d7\u211d), maps each leaf (a,b) to M.mul(a,b)=M.round(ab), preserving the ordered shape. Writing P_M for this construction, P_M(leaf((a,b)))=leaf(M.round(ab)) and P_M(node(l,r))=node(P_M(l),P_M(r)). Each multiplication is exact real multiplication followed by one application of the model rounding function. The definition does not round the factors first and performs no additions. Its output is the real tree passed to roundedSum in roundedDotProduct."
    },
    {
      "name": "Research.BinaryTreeReduction.roundedDotProduct",
      "description": "A function (M,t)\u21a6roundedSum(M,roundedProductTree(M,t)), with M any model and t\u2208Tree(\u211d\u00d7\u211d), returning a real number. For any real tree, roundedSum(M,leaf(x))=x and roundedSum(M,node(l,r))=M.add(roundedSum(M,l),roundedSum(M,r))=M.round(roundedSum(M,l)+roundedSum(M,r)). Equivalently D_M(leaf((a,b)))=M.round(ab) and D_M(node(l,r))=M.round(D_M(l)+D_M(r)). Thus it rounds each leaf product once and each internal addition once, follows the existing tree grouping without rebalancing, and does not apply another round at a leaf during summation. Under the target theorem\u2019s hypothesis, this scalar equals the exact sum of an existential same-shape tree of perturbed exact products."
    }
  ],
